    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        { "t": "text", "v": "Every one of the " },
        { "t": "strong", "v": "30 task statements" },
        { "t": "text", "v": " is covered by at least one scenario guide. Where a statement appears in two guides (1.7, 2.2, 4.2, 4.5, 5.1, 5.4), each guide approaches it from a different scenario — read both treatments; the exam does the same." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        { "t": "text", "v": "A reading order that follows the weights: " },
        { "t": "strong", "v": "01 → 03 → 04" },
        { "t": "text", "v": " first (Domains 1 + 2, 45%), then " },
        { "t": "strong", "v": "02 → 05" },
        { "t": "text", "v": " (Domain 3 plus the review half of Domain 4), then " },
        { "t": "strong", "v": "06" },
        { "t": "text", "v": " (extraction and reliability)." }
      ]
    },
    { "type": "divider" },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "spans": [{ "t": "text", "v": "3. Cross-Cutting Answer Patterns (Learn These Cold)" }]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        { "t": "text", "v": "The official sample questions reveal a consistent grading philosophy. When two answers both \"could work,\" the correct one almost always follows one of these principles — each anchored to the task statements where the exam tests it:" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": true,
      "items": [
        {
          "spans": [
            { "t": "strong", "v": "Deterministic > probabilistic when compliance is mandatory." },
            { "t": "text", "v": " Business-critical ordering (verify identity before refund) → programmatic hooks/gates, not prompt text or few-shot examples. Prompts have a non-zero failure rate. " },
            { "t": "em", "v": "(task statements 1.4, 1.5)" }
          ],
          "marker": "none",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "spans": [
            { "t": "strong", "v": "Fix the root cause with the simplest proportionate change." },
            { "t": "text", "v": " Bad tool selection → improve tool " },
            { "t": "em", "v": "descriptions" },
            { "t": "text", "v": " first, before few-shot examples, routing layers, or classifiers. \"Over-engineered\" is a recurring wrong-answer trap. " },
            { "t": "em", "v": "(task statement 2.1)" }
          ],
          "marker": "none",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "spans": [
            { "t": "strong", "v": "Self-reported confidence and sentiment are unreliable proxies." },
            { "t": "text", "v": " Escalation should be driven by explicit criteria + few-shot examples, not confidence thresholds or sentiment analysis. " },
            { "t": "em", "v": "(task statements 5.2, 5.5)" }
          ],
          "marker": "none",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "spans": [
            { "t": "strong", "v": "Match the API to latency tolerance." },
            { "t": "text", "v": " Batch API (50% cheaper, up to 24h, no SLA) for overnight/weekly jobs; synchronous API for anything blocking. " },
            { "t": "em", "v": "(task statement 4.5)" }
          ],
          "marker": "none",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "spans": [
            { "t": "strong", "v": "Attention dilutes; split the work." },
            { "t": "text", "v": " Large multi-file reviews → per-file passes + a separate cross-file integration pass. Bigger context windows do NOT fix attention quality. " },
            { "t": "em", "v": "(task statements 1.6, 4.6)" }
          ],
          "marker": "none",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "spans": [
            { "t": "strong", "v": "Independent reviewers beat self-review." },
            { "t": "text", "v": " A fresh Claude instance without the generator's reasoning context catches more issues. " },
            { "t": "em", "v": "(task statements 3.6, 4.6)" }
          ],
          "marker": "none",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "spans": [
            { "t": "strong", "v": "Never fabricate; make schemas honest." },
            { "t": "text", "v": " Nullable/optional fields where data may be absent; enums with \"unclear\"/\"other\"+detail; retries can't recover information that isn't in the source. " },
            { "t": "em", "v": "(task statements 4.3, 4.4)" }
          ],
          "marker": "none",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "spans": [
            { "t": "strong", "v": "Errors must carry structure." },
            { "t": "text", "v": " errorCategory, isRetryable, what was attempted, partial results. Generic failures, silent suppression, and whole-workflow termination are all anti-patterns. " },
            { "t": "em", "v": "(task statements 2.2, 5.3)" }
          ],
          "marker": "none",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "spans": [
            { "t": "strong", "v": "Subagents share nothing by default." },
            { "t": "text", "v": " Context must be passed explicitly in prompts; coordinator routes all communication (hub-and-spoke). " },
            { "t": "em", "v": "(task statements 1.2, 1.3)" }
          ],
          "marker": "none",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "spans": [
            { "t": "strong", "v": "Preserve provenance." },
            { "t": "text", "v": " Claim–source mappings, publication dates, conflict annotation with attribution — never silently pick one value. " },
            { "t": "em", "v": "(task statements 1.3, 5.6)" }
          ],
          "marker": "none",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    { "type": "divider" },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "spans": [{ "t": "text", "v": "4. What the Exam Does Not Test" }]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        { "t": "text", "v": "The Official Exam Guide's out-of-scope list is worth taking literally — every hour spent on it is an hour taken from a 27% domain. Nothing on the exam covers: " },
        { "t": "strong", "v": "model internals" },
        { "t": "text", "v": " (Claude's training, Constitutional AI/RLHF, tokenization, prompt-caching internals, benchmarking); " },
        { "t": "strong", "v": "adjacent ML infrastructure" },
        { "t": "text", "v": " (fine-tuning, embeddings, vector databases); " },
        { "t": "strong", "v": "API operations" },
        { "t": "text", "v": " (auth/billing, rate limits and pricing math, OAuth/key rotation, streaming); " },
        { "t": "strong", "v": "deployment plumbing" },
        { "t": "text", "v": " (MCP server hosting/infrastructure, cloud-provider specifics); the " },
        { "t": "strong", "v": "multimodal surface" },
        { "t": "text", "v": " (vision, computer use); or language/framework internals. One nuance: the Batch API's 50% discount is still fair game — it drives an architecture decision, not pricing arithmetic. If a practice resource drills anything else on this list, it is preparing you for a different exam." }
      ]
    },
